meta { title = "Strings and Unicode", description = "Work with immutable UTF-8 text and scalar-based indexes.", order = 5, group = "Language reference" }

String is immutable, well-formed UTF-8 text. Use double-quoted literals and + for concatenation. Numbers and Boolean values are not implicitly formatted as Strings.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn greeting(name: String) -> String:
  "Hello, " + name

fn main() -> String:
  greeting("Kalandra")
""" }

h2 { "Length is measured in Unicode scalars" }

length(text) returns a count of Unicode scalar values; byteLength(text) counts UTF-8 bytes. Neither is a count of user-perceived grapheme clusters. Combining marks can contribute additional scalars.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> int:
  text = "سلام"
  length(text) + byteLength(text)
""" }

h2 { "Index and slice" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> String:
  text = "Kalandra"
  slice(text, 0, 3) + text[7]
""" }

Indexes are zero-based scalar indexes. slice uses a half-open start/end range, with both endpoints checked; it does not clamp or wrap. An indexed scalar is returned as a String, not a separate Char type.

h2 { "Equality and ownership" }

== and != compare exact scalar sequences. No normalization or locale-dependent collation is inserted. Strings can be retained in CPU workers, closures, arrays and supported aggregate fields. String execution is not part of the GPU numerical subset.

Escapes include a quoted double quote, a backslash, newline, carriage return, tab, zero and Unicode-scalar escapes. Invalid UTF-8 and invalid escapes are diagnosed. String interpolation, raw multiline strings and general text parsing are not supported.

